.TH RXEDOT 1 "Aug 2026" Linux "User Manuals"
.SH NAME
rxedot \- draw the parse tree of a regular expression as Graphviz DOT
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rxedot
[\fIoptions\fR]
.I regex
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rxedot
parses
.I regex
with the same library
.BR rxenum (1)
uses, then walks the finished tree and writes a Graphviz graph of it on
standard output. Pipe it into
.BR dot (1):
.PP
.RS
.B rxedot '([2-9TJQKA][SHDC]){{5}}' | dot -Tpng -o hand.png
.RE
.PP
The library itself knows nothing of DOT;
.B rxedot
is a sibling that reads the same public structure. Because a repetition is
counted rather than written out, the tree stays small even for astronomically
large sets: a fixed run is one node, not one per position.
.PP
Every box carries its cardinality, so the arithmetic of the set is on show: a
concatenation multiplies its children, an alternation sums them, a
combinatorial
.B {{k}}
is C(n,k) or P(n,k). With the numbers in place the tree becomes a device you
can run by hand \(en see FOLLOWING A PATH below \(en and
.B \-f
runs it for you.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-f index
Light the path taken to reach one member. The tree is sought to
.BR index ,
a decimal from zero, and the chosen branch at each alternation, together with
every node on the way, is drawn in a heavier coloured outline. A title names
the index and the member it reaches.
.TP
.B \-c
Force a
.B (?N)
subroutine to be drawn collapsed \(en as a single node with a dashed edge back
to the group it copies. Compact, but a path through it is ambiguous, since the
several calls share the one drawn group.
.TP
.B \-e
Force a subroutine to be drawn in full, each call its own copy. Clearer under
.BR \-f ,
where the calls go their own ways. Without either flag the default is
collapsed, but expanded when
.B \-f
is given.
.TP
.B \-u n
Unroll a fixed
.B {k}
repetition into
.B k
copies of its body when
.B k
is at most
.BR n ;
the default is 0, which never unrolls. A repetition left rolled up is still
one node, but under
.B \-f
it lists what each iteration chose beside it (capped and ellipsised, so a large
count costs nothing). Only fixed, finite repetitions unroll or list; variable,
open-ended, and infinite ones are left alone.
.TP
.B \-t
Omit the title. The input regex is shown above the tree by default; with
.B \-f
the index and member are shown too. This hides the regex line; the
.B \-f
line, when present, remains.
.TP
.B \-w
Draw each fixed single character as its own leaf, rather than folding a run of
them into one word node. Explicit, at the cost of width.
.SH READING THE TREE
.TP
.B Cardinality
Every node's second line is the number of members of the set it stands for,
abbreviated past fifteen digits. A node with no largest member \(en an infinite
one \(en shows the infinity sign and is ringed.
.TP
.B Character classes and literals
A leaf shows the exact text it was parsed from:
.BR [a-z] ,
.BR \ed ,
a literal character, a
.B [:name:]
dictionary. A run of two or more fixed single characters is folded into one
word node, so
.B cat
reads whole rather than as three boxes; see
.B \-w
to draw them apart.
.TP
.B Alternation
Several alternatives become one rounded record, a subsection per branch.
Each shows where that branch starts in the numbering and, after a
.BR + ,
how many it holds. The branches lie end to end, so a subsection's start plus
its size is the next one's start \(en which is how a seek picks a branch.
.TP
.B Concatenation
Nodes in sequence multiply. A node that carries \(en whose members are not all
the same \(en shows its place value as
.BR "\(mu weight" :
how far the index moves when that digit ticks over. It is the divide-step for
peeling the digit off an index.
.TP
.B Repetition
A
.B {m,n}
repetition is one node over a single copy of its body. See
.B \-u
for drawing the iterations out.
.TP
.B Subroutine and backreference
A
.B (?N)
subroutine and a
.B \eN
backreference both draw a dashed edge to the group they point at. See
.B \-c
and
.BR \-e .
.SH FOLLOWING A PATH
The numbers turn the tree into a working odometer. To find the member at an
index by hand, start at the set and descend:
.IP \(bu 3
at an alternation, choose the subsection whose start is the greatest not
exceeding the index, and subtract that start from it;
.IP \(bu 3
at a concatenation, at each node that shows
.BR "\(mu weight" ,
divide the index by the weight and take the remainder as the smaller digits go
on \(en the quotient selects this node's member;
.IP \(bu 3
a leaf is reached with a single value; read it off.
.PP
.B \-f
does exactly this and lights the route, so the two can be checked against each
other. For a finite, place-value set the numbers and the path are exact; an
infinite set is ordered by length instead of place value and is left unlit.
.PP
The numbers describe the natural place-value order only. A Feistel keyed
permutation reorders members by an arithmetic they do not express:
.BR rxenum (1)'s
.B \-k
has no counterpart here, so
.BR rxedot 's
index is always the unpermuted one, and a
.B (?~key:re)
shuffle within the tree is not followable by hand \(en though
.BR \-f ,
which performs the real seek, still reaches the right member across one.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B rxedot '\ed{4}-\ed{2}-\ed{2}' | dot -Tsvg -o date.svg
The tree of a date, each field a repetition with its place value.
.TP
.B rxedot -f 20240315 '\ed{4}-\ed{2}-\ed{2}' | dot -Tpng | display
The path to
.BR 2024-03-15 ,
with each field listing the digits it chose.
.TP
.B rxedot -e -u 3 -f 5 '(a|b){3}' | dot -Tpng -o unrolled.png
The three iterations drawn out, each lighting the branch it took.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rxenum (1),
.BR dot (1)
